 \begin{abstract}
-Under the standard routing that assigns Muon the hidden weight matrices and AdamW the embeddings and output head, Muon reaches the grokking threshold on modular arithmetic in fewer steps. The solutions it reaches do not hold: every configuration that groks subsequently loses generalization, with training accuracy failing alongside test, and AdamW pushed to larger step sizes collapses too. We localize the failure to the representation--readout interface, which the residual stream identifies only jointly, up to an invertible map the loss does not select. Branching from bit-identical states, freezing either side prevents the imminent collapse, and anchoring the embeddings and readout after the circuit forms eliminates it on every seed. Filtering the representation to the Fourier family the task selects separates \emph{circuit failure}, where the family stops computing, from \emph{circuit masking}, where the family solves the task perfectly in isolation and is outvoted by the rest of the representation; rescaling the family alone restores the model, and grokking is the same competition resolving upward. At the step the model stops computing the task, the support and power measures that certify grokked circuits report an intact circuit.
+Post-grokking collapse can destroy accuracy while preserving decodable task information. We study modular-arithmetic transformers trained with Muon on hidden weights and AdamW on embeddings and the output head. At five captured transitions, adjacent swaps localize the acute failure to the head update. Its mean-dependent logit term reproduces each failing test accuracy within $0.33$ percentage points. Training-only linear decoders recover $98.20$--$100\%$ at collapsed checkpoints. Corrected cross-entropy prevents joint train/test collapse through step $100{,}000$ in five matched branches; an RMS-normalized model still fails through an embedding update. The captured updates locally descend accurate loss and overshoot at full scale. Finally, task-aligned Fourier filtering averages logits over inputs sharing the answer, allowing a memorizer to achieve perfect filtered accuracy.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
 
-Mechanistic accounts of grokking rest on two progress measures: the set of Fourier frequencies a circuit occupies and the fraction of representational power they carry \citep{nanda2023progress}. Together they are the standard certificate that a circuit is present. We exhibit a regime in which the certificate and the function come apart. At the step a grokked transformer stops computing modular addition, the frequency support of the family that computes the task is identical to its pre-collapse state and its power distribution retains a cosine similarity of $0.9899$ (Figure~\ref{fig:collapse}). Both measures are invariant under a change of basis of the residual stream: support under any invertible map, and power under any orthogonal one. The failure we study moves along this freedom: the spectral certificate is unchanged while the compatibility between representation and readout fails, and measures of the function register it in full.
+Generalization acquired during grokking can later collapse. We follow individual failures in modular-arithmetic transformers to identify the damaging update and determine what information survives. At a captured failure, dominant-frequency membership is unchanged and the task-family power distribution retains cosine similarity $0.9899$ (Figure~\ref{fig:collapse}). Fresh decoders recover high accuracy from collapsed representations.
 
